- Application:    http://127.0.0.1:3000/
- Prometheus:     http://127.0.0.1:9090/
- Grafana:        http://127.0.0.1:3001/
- Kibana:         http://127.0.0.1:5601/
- Elasticsearch:  http://127.0.0.1:9200/
- API Gateway:    http://127.0.0.1:8080/

The React and TypeScript frontend calls the API gateway. The gateway checks
the JWT and routes requests to Spring Boot services. The transaction service
reads the demo user's records from Supabase Postgres; the overview status is
based on a successful summary API request

Explain the login flow: the frontend sends credentials to the auth API;
   auth verifies the stored password using BCrypt and returns a signed JWT.
   The frontend sends the token as a Bearer token on protected API calls.


Point out that the gateway JWT filter allows login/register and actuator
   paths through, and requires a valid signed token for application routes.
   It removes any client-provided X-User-Id and sets X-User-Id from the
   verified token claim. This prevents a caller from choosing another user
   simply by changing that header.

xplain that the Supabase database password and shared JWT signing secret
   are supplied to backend containers from environment configuration. The
   frontend receives neither secret. Terraform state can contain sensitive
   values, so keep it private and never commit or casually share it.

For AWS, show the Terraform security groups in the plan/code. The minimal
   deployment opens its frontend HTTP port publicly, keeps the database in
   private subnets, and permits the EC2 host outbound traffic. Explain that
   public HTTP is not HTTPS and that this learning stack has no ALB/TLS/WAF.

